---
// Who and what this benchmark is built on. Model makers come from the export (model_key's organisation), so the
// list grows with the ladder; everything else is the software and work every battle depends on.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { allModels, isModel } from '../lib/data';
import { routes } from '../lib/routes';

const llms = (await allModels()).filter((m) => isModel(m) && m.model_key);
const makers = Object.entries(
  llms.reduce<Record<string, string[]>>((acc, m) => {
    const org = m.model_key!.split('/')[0];
    (acc[org] ??= []).push(m.display_name);
    return acc;
  }, {}),
).sort(([a], [b]) => a.localeCompare(b));
---
<Base title="Credits" description="The people and software RotomArena is built on.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Acknowledgements</p>
  <h1>Credits</h1>
  <p class="prose">
    RotomArena is a small layer on top of other people's work: a battle engine, a Python bridge to it, a search agent
    from a research paper, and the models themselves. This page says who made what, and what we use it for.
  </p>

  <div class="credits">
    <section class="hud-panel card">
      <h2>PokéChamp</h2>
      <p class="who">Seth Karten, Andy Luu Nguyen and Chi Jin · ICML 2025</p>
      <p>
        A minimax agent with a language model inside its search. Our reference bot implements their design from the
        paper's description, with hand-written rules where the original uses a model; it contains none of their code.
        It is a fixed yardstick, not a reproduction of their agent. <a href={routes.model('pokechamp')}>How we use it</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="links">
        <a href="https://sites.google.com/view/pokechamp-llm">Project</a>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.04094">Paper</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/sethkarten/pokechamp">Code</a>
        <a href="https://pokeagent.github.io/">PokéAgent Challenge</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="hud-panel card">
      <h2>Pokémon Showdown</h2>
      <p class="who">Guangcong Luo and contributors · MIT license</p>
      <p>
        Every battle on this site runs on a local Pokémon Showdown server: its engine applies the rules, damage,
        abilities, items and randomness, and its Gen 9 OU format supplies the clauses. The replay player on each
        match page, with its sprites and battle effects, loads from play.pokemonshowdown.com.
      </p>
      <p class="links">
        <a href="https://pokemonshowdown.com/">Website</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/smogon/pokemon-showdown">Code</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="hud-panel card">
      <h2>poke-env</h2>
      <p class="who">Haris Sahovic and contributors · MIT license</p>
      <p>
        The Python library that connects our players to Showdown: it reads the battle protocol into battle state,
        lists the legal actions every player chooses from, and sends their choices back.
      </p>
      <p class="links"><a href="https://github.com/hsahovic/poke-env">Code</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="hud-panel card">
      <h2>Smogon</h2>
      <p class="who">The Smogon competitive community</p>
      <p>
        The OverUsed tier our battles are played in, its banlist and clauses, and the team export format our teams
        are written in, are Smogon's. Our reference teams come from Smogon's SV OU sample-team thread, and each one's
        builder is named on the <a href={routes.teams}>Teams</a> page. The fixed-teams benchmark's two teams are older: the AI assistant
        that scaffolded this project wrote them from memory, so no builder can be named.
      </p>
      <p class="links"><a href="https://www.smogon.com/">Website</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="hud-panel card">
      <h2>The models</h2>
      <p class="who">Open weights, unless noted on their pages</p>
      <p>Every rated model is somebody's work. So far:</p>
      <ul class="makers">
        {makers.map(([org, names]) => (<li><code>{org}</code> {names.join(', ')}</li>))}
      </ul>
      <p class="small muted">
        Local models run on <a href="https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp">llama.cpp</a> (MIT), Bonsai on
        <a href="https://github.com/PrismML-Eng/llama.cpp">Prism ML's fork</a>; API models through
        <a href="https://openrouter.ai/">OpenRouter</a>. Each model's page credits its makers and links their work.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="hud-panel card">
      <h2>Methods</h2>
      <p class="who">Ideas we use, with thanks</p>
      <p>
        Ratings are a Bradley-Terry model (Bradley and Terry, 1952) on the Elo scale (Elo, 1978); pairwise ratings
        for language models were popularized by Chatbot Arena (Chiang et al., 2024). Full references are in the
        <a href={routes.paper}>paper</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="hud-panel card">
      <h2>This site</h2>
      <p class="who">Built with Astro · hosted on Netlify</p>
      <p>
        Typeset in Inter, Rajdhani and JetBrains Mono, served by Google Fonts under the SIL Open Font License.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our code is <a href="https://opensource.org/license/mit-0">MIT-0</a>; the games, ratings, teams and this site's
        text are <a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/">CC0</a>. Take anything, no credit needed.
        Smogon's reference teams stay their builders', and Pokémon stays Nintendo's.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <p class="prose small muted">
    Missing someone? If we use your work and have not said so here, that is our mistake.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .credits { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 22rem), 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 1.8rem 0; }
  .card { padding: 1.1rem 1.3rem; }
  .card h2 { margin: 0 0 0.2rem; font-size: 1.4rem; }
  .who { margin: 0 0 0.6rem; font: 600 0.75rem/1.4 var(--font-display); letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--cyan); }
  .card p { font-size: 0.94rem; }
  .links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.3rem 1rem; font: 600 0.85rem var(--font-display); letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .makers { padding-left: 1.1rem; font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .makers li { margin: 0.25rem 0; }
</style>
